hey everyone this is kevin from the  chess website comm and today we're gonna  be looking at a very very solid defense  in the queen's indian defense and it  starts out with pawn to d4 Knight to f6  simple development moom trying to  control the center pawn to c4 and pawn  to e6 from black now a lot of times here  you will see players bring their Knight  to c3 very good place for this Knight  and simple development move but this  comes into the nimzo-indian defense and  a lot of white players don't like to  play this because a lot of times they'll  have doubled pawns and even top players  a lot of GM's will try to avoid the  nimzo-indian defense and so instead of  playing Knight to c3 they instead play  Knight to f3 and what this does is you  can see a few things  white would really like to control the  e4 square he'd realize he can't pushes  his pawn forward right away because our  knight on f6 sits here and it defends  this square and so he would really like  to play his Knight to c3 right away and  defend this pawn that he could push  forward to e4 but as we talked about  before a lot of players don't like to  play the nimzo-indian defense so this  brings us to the queen zenith indian  defense and this allows black in this  case to bring his pawn to b6 getting  ready to fianchetto his bishop to this  b7 square and try to gain access and  gain control of these light squares as  you can see here black already controls  this e4 and d5 square with his knight  here and so this is another hyper modern  defense where blacks really looking to  have a very solid defense and at the  same time control the center with his  minor pieces now why it does have a few  options here and so we're gonna take a  look at some of the most popular ones  that you will see but more times than  not that the move that you're going to  see from white to combat our b6 move is  white playing pawn to g3 and he does  this because it's very very important  for him not to just give up these light  squares  it's very important for a white to at  least try and hold them off by means of  Bishop to g2 eventually doesn't have to  do that right away but you really don't  want to just concede this entire  LightSquared diagonal to black because  that really gives black the opportunity  to counter-attack anytime he wants to  so this is a very properly move that you  will see from white and now here black  actually has two main options one I  prefer but but two that you'll see a lot  and the first one is the old line and I  don't prefer it too much but you will  see a lot of play with it and that's  Bishop to b7 this is really more of a  passive game I know it he is on this  long diagonal and he's trying to control  this but it is passive compared to his  second move that we'll talk about here  in just a minute but from here the  LightSquared Bishop for white can come  to g2 again contesting these light  squares that we have here Black's really  trying to hold on to this square on e4  he really doesn't want white to be able  to push his pawn to e4 and just have a  complete dominant center control here  from here black can simply bring his  Bishop to e7 again Black's playing very  passive but keep in mind there is a lot  of variations in the Queen's in the  defense that we'll talk about the black  actually has a lot of options to not  play as passive then he can play a  little bit more aggressively but in this  variation if he does want to just  solidify this light squares then he can  bring his bishop to b7 and then bring  his Bishop to e7 and then both sides are  going to castle on the King side and  from here White's just going to continue  his development here with Knight to c3  as you can see here now our Bishop  coming to b4 is it with an Enzo in the  in defense it's really not going to hold  down it's not going to lock down this  this Knight pin to the King because the  King  is castled on the kingside shows you can  see right here a fairly normal game that  you'll see in the Queen's Indian defense  and the old line where the bishop came  to b7 the White King is very very well  protected in this look nice little Kings  Indian defense if you will if you're  familiar the queens and kings in the in  defense it's kind of have that defense  going on at the same time we have our  LightSquared Bishop is very good but our  King is safe but really White has a lot  of sinner control here so this this is  an option so so keep this in mind but  the second option is actually Bishop to  a6 and that's kind of want to want to  talk about a little bit more because  that's what I recommend and I've had a  little bit more success with that one  myself so we'll go ahead and get into  that next variation that I do want to  look at is Bishop to a6 now again the  traditional play is Bishop to b7 but  actually think that Bishop excuse me  Bishop to a6 it's a much better move it  definitely is more aggressive so if  you're if you're a more aggressive  player like myself I think that Bishop  to a6 is going to be a much powerful  move so you can see here we're attacking  this pawn on c4 and white really doesn't  have a lot of good options to defend it  he doesn't want to bring his knight to  d2 it's not a very good place for his  knight he really wants to bring his  Knight to c3 eventually at the same time  he's blocking off his Queen which he  does not want to do if he brings his  Queen to c2 which you'll see a lot of  people want to do then he's really not  paying a lot of attention to this pawn  on d4 which right now since white really  is paying attention to these dark  squares he's trying to control the dark  squares and not the light squares with  this pawn on d4 his queen is no longer  defending it so from here white can  really try to take advantage of this  pawn on d4 now a lot of people say Kevin  why can't we just bring our pawn to e3  simple discovery defense on the c4 pawn  but you can see here what's defending  this pawn on c4 it's this bishop on f1  and since white played pawn to d3 he  really wants to fianchetto his bishop to  g2 there's no sense of playing g3 if  he's going  have this bishop on f1 defending this  pawn because as soon as you see and  kiddos then he's gonna have to have  another defender come to defend this c4  pawn so instead the most common move  that you will see is pawn to b3 and from  here black and play Bishop to b4 now  this is very important because before as  we talked about or Knight to c3 is no  longer possible from white but a lot of  people if they get the moves mixed up  instead of Bishop to a6 if they bring  their Bishop to b4 first now Knight to  c3 is possible now a lot of white  players again like to avoid this because  they don't like double pawns but you can  see here this is a little bit different  variation because the pond here is on b2  so it's very important that we one bring  out our Bishop d6 to attack this pawn  but also to get this pawn to b3 so it's  out of the way and we're bringing our  bishop now to b4 then the knight can no  longer come to c3 because then we'll  just capture it and go up three points  and just GG white there so from here  White has two options and the worst  option for him is Knight to d2 and this  is actually going to be a trap that I'm  gonna make a video on just here in a  minute but the second move and the best  option is to bring his Bishop to d2 and  so if you are playing as white this is  your best option if you do see  however Knight coming to d2 make sure  you check out the trap video and see  what to do there but for time restraints  I'm gonna go ahead and just get into a  few other options here but just keep in  mind that if what you really want to do  is just attack attack attack in my  opinion so I think that a six really  gives you that opportunity now you can  also bring your pawn to b5 if you really  want to keep in mind black soft options  here if you look at it you really don't  want to fianchetto both sides we can  castle on the King side if we want to  now obviously if he plays this Knight to  d2  then we're going to lay our track but if  he brings his Bishop to d2 and we want  to retreat and bring our Bishop back to  e7  we can still castle in the Kings side we  have a lot of counter play that we can  have we can bring your pawn to c5 we can  bring our pawn to d5 if we really wanted  to we could bring our pawn to b5 but as  you can see this is a little bit more  aggressive play from black so black does  have some options if he does come to a6  and I've had a lot more success playing  as black with a6 than b7 that's partly  just because I'm more aggressive than  passive but you can play around with  both of them and see what fits you best  so we're going to take a look at a few  more variations more importantly if  white does not bring his pawn to g3  right away in the beginning the next  variation is the Petrosian variation  it's pawn to a3 as you can see White's  avoiding this pawn to g3 Fink heading on  the kingside and instead he really wants  to bring his Knight to c3 but he doesn't  want to have to worry about this Bishop  on b4 pinning down his Knights at the  king so instead he's gonna play pawn to  a3 now a lot of people will say go ahead  black and go in fee and carry your  Bishop and you know just play normally  but I'm a very aggressive player and I  like to play aggressive lines so in this  case I've actually had a lot of success  with pawn to c5 now a lot of times from  here white we'll just come down with  this pawn to d5 and now we can bring our  Bishop to a6 attacking this weak point  in with this pawn on c4 now if white  tries to capture and just comes down  then we're gonna take with our F pawn  and take on e6 and as you can see here  this is a little bit different  eventually we can push our pawn forward  with pawn to d5 and we have an extra  defender here that we can recapture with  and this is gonna be a very aggressive  variation in the Queen's Indian defense  for black once we get our Bishop  involved and we castle on the King side  this semi-open F file is going to be  very good for a rook  and as you can see your white has a very  solid game but this is gonna be a very  aggressive line for black so if you'd  like to mix things up if you don't want  to just play for a draw and you are  playing the Quin Queens in the in the  fence then I do definitely recommend  this and the last variation that we'll  talk about real quick is just Knight to  c3 and from here blacks gonna play  Bishop to b4 and this is the  nimzo-indian offense that you'll see so  I'll make another video on the  nimzo-indian defense but that's kind of  what this is based off of so hope you  guys enjoyed the video make sure you  check out the website and I'll see you  guys in the next video thanks for  watching

